������� ������������������ ���������������� ������������������ ���������������������� �������� ���������������� ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ������ ������ ������������������ ���������������������� ���������� ���������������������� ������������������ ��������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ������ �������� �������������������� ���������� ������������������������ ������������������ ������������������ �������������� ��������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������\n��\n���������������������������� ������������������ �������������������� ������ ���������������������� ���������������� �������� �������� �������������� ��������\n������������������������ ������ ������������������ �������������������� ���������������� ���������������� ���� ����������������������\n���������������� ���������������������������� ������������ ������������������ ���������������������� �������������� ��������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������ �������������������� ������������������ ������ ���������������� �������� ������ ������������ ������ ��������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_279": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-279\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21/07/2023\n“PROMOTION OF AYURVEDAAND YOGA”\n279: SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government subscribes to the view that the country needs to stay authentic\nfor the international market, and continue to promote Ayurveda and yoga, and keep the entry\nrequirements to India consistent;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the initiatives taken/proposed to be taken by the\nGovernment in this regard; and\n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) and (b) Yes, The Ministry of Ayush has adopted several proactive ‘product and services’\nstrategy to stay authentic in the international as well as domestic market.\nThe Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India (API) is a comprehensive document that contains\nstandards for the quality, safety, and efficacy of Ayurvedic drugs. It provides guidelines for\nthe identification, purity, and strength of Ayurvedic ingredients, formulations, and finished\nproducts. The API is developed and regularly updated by the Ministry of Ayush in\ncollaboration with the experts in the field.\nCertificate of Pharmaceutical Products (COPP) as per World Health Organization- Good\nManufacturing Practices (WHO GMP) Certification Scheme is granted by the Central Drugs\nStandard Control Organization (CDSCO). It certifies that a particular pharmaceutical product,\nincluding Ayurvedic medicines, has been manufactured in accordance with the relevant\nguidelines and meets the quality standards required for its registration and sale in India and\ninternational markets. The COPP is an important requirement for obtaining marketing\nauthorizations and exporting pharmaceutical products.\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) has developed benchmark standards for the training\nof Ayurveda \u0000which standardizes the adequate training of practitioners globally.\nWHO also has standards for assessing quality of herbal medicines. These standards provide\nguidelines for the quality and safety of herbal medicines, including Ayurvedic products\n\nTo certify yoga practices, the Yoga Certification Board (YCB) was established by the\nMinistry of Ayush, Government of India. The YCB aims to standardize the certification\nprocess for yoga professionals, ensuring they meet set standards for education, training, and\ncompetence. By assessing knowledge, skills, and adherence to standardized practices, the\nYCB promotes quality control and provides certification to qualified yoga professionals.\nThe Ministry of Ayush, to further this initiative, developed the central sector scheme for\nPromotion of International Cooperation for Ayush. Under this scheme the Ministry provides\nsupport to Indian Ayush drug Manufacturers/ Ayush Service providers to give boost to the\nexport of Ayush products and services; facilitates the International promotion, development\nand recognition of Ayush systems of medicine; foster interaction of stakeholders and market\ndevelopment of Ayush at international level; promote academics and research through the\nestablishment of Ayush Academic Chairs in foreign countries and holding training\nworkshop/symposiums for promoting and strengthening awareness and interest about Ayush\nSystems of Medicine at international level.\nSeveral initiatives undertaken during 2021-22 & 2022-23 by the Ministry such as inclusion of\n‘Traditional Medicine’ chapter in the International Classification of Diseases (ICD); Draft\nFood Safety and Standards (Ayurveda Aahar) Regulation, 2021; emphasis on scientific\nresearch; Ayush Export Promotion Council (AYUSHEXCIL); WHO global Centre for\nTraditional Medicine; yoga as competitive sport with formation of International Yogasana\nSports Federation; Yogasana announced as sport; creation of a Strategic Policy and\nFacilitation Bureau, etc.\nIn addition, the Ministry of Ayush has taken the following steps towards the international\nrecognition for Ayush systems of medicine:\n• Signed 24 Country to Country MoUs for Cooperation in field of Traditional\nMedicine and Homoeopathy with foreign nations.\n• Signed 46 MoUs with international institutes for undertaking Collaborative\nResearch / Academic collaboration.\n• Signed 15 MoUs with international institutes for setting up of Ayush Academic\nChairs in foreign nations.\n• Supported the establishment of 39 Ayush Information Cells in 35 foreign nations.\n• Offers scholarships to foreign nationals for studying Ayush courses in recognized\nAyush institutions in India under International Ayush Fellowship/Scholarship\nProgramme.\n(c) Not Applicable\n********",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_280": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *280 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08TH AUGUST, 2023 \n \nREARING OF DOMESTIC LIVESTOCK \n \n*280:  SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL:  \nSHRI DURGA DAS (D.D.) UIKEY: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of efforts being made by the Government to promote rearing of domestic \nlivestock in the country including tribal areas;  \n(b) the total funds provided by the Government to promote rearing of domestic livestock in \nrural areas and tribal areas in the  country including Madhya Pradesh during the last three \nyears; and \n(c) the details of steps taken by the Government for the conservation and maintenance of \nindigenous/ native breed of cows? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF LOK SABHA QUESTION NO. * 280 \nPART (a) TO ( c) FOR ANSWER ON 08.08.2023 ASKED BY SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH \nPATEL: SHRI DURGA DAS (D.D.) UIKEY:  REGARDING“REARING OF DOMESTIC \nLIVESTOCK” \n \n(a) The Government is implementing  various scheme sfor promotion  and development  of \nanimal husbandry including rearing of domestic livestock in the country including tribal \nareas such as National Livestock Mission (NLM), RashtriyaGokul Mission (RGM), \nNational Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD), Dairy Processing and \nInfrastructure Development Fund (DIDF), Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer \nProducer Organizations engaged in dairy activities (SDCFPO) , Livestock Health and \nDisease Control Programme (LH&DCP). \n \n(b) The Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Government of India has \nreleasedfunds under various scheme sto the States/UTsincluding Madhya Pradesh in the last 3 \nyears as under:  \n(Rupees in Crores) \nYear 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nAll India including \nMadhya Pradesh 1780.29 2293.40 1132.20 \nMadhya Pradesh 87.17 132.71 92.90 \n \n(c) The Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying is implementing RashtriyaGokul \nMission for development and conservation of indigenous bovine breeds since December \n2014. The scheme is important in enhancing milk production and productivity of bovines \nto meet growing demand of milk and making dairying more remunerative t o the rural \nfarmers of the country.   \n \nThe major steps undertaken for the development and conservation of indigenous breeds \nunder RGM are as follows: \n  \n(i) Nationwide Artificial Insemination  program for extension of Artificial \nInsemination coverage \n(ii) Progeny testing and pedigree selection for production of high genetic m erit bulls \nof indigenous breeds \n(iii) Establishment of Breed Multiplication Farms preferably of indigenous breeds \n(iv) Promotion of In -vitro Fertilization (IVF) technology for faster genetic \nupgradation of indigenous breeds \n(v) Implementation of genomic selection using genomic chip \n(vi) Sex sorted semen production for indigenous breeds \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_281": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 281 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.08.2023 \n \nEXPANSION OF RAILWAY NETWORK \n \n†*281.  MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA:   \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to expand railway network \nand run new trains in West Bengal, Jammu & Kashmir, Bihar \nand Madhya Pradesh; \n  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to start Vande Bharat Train \nfrom Bhagalpur Railway Station or to include the said Station \non the route of Agartala Rajdhani Express; \n \n(d)  if so, the project -wise total amount of funds allocated for new \nrail projects to West Bengal , Jammu & Kashmir, Bihar and \nMadhya Pradesh during the last five years and current year; \n \n(e)  whether the Government proposes to connect Bhind and Datia \ndistricts to any other railway line and also connect the said \ndistricts to Delhi and Bhopal; and \n \n(f)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f ) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 281 BY MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE AND \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA \nON 09.08.2023 REGARDING EXPANSION OF RAILWAY NETWORK \n \n(a) to (d)  : The Railway projects are sanctioned/executed Zonal \nRailway wise and not State wise as the Railways’ projects may span \nacross State boundaries /District boundaries.  Sanctioning of \nRailway projects is a continuous and dynamic process of Indian \nRailway. Railway Infrastructure Projects are taken up on the basis \nof remunerativeness, las t mile connectivity, missing links and \nalternate routes, augmentation of congested/saturated lines socio -\neconomic considerations etc. depending upon liabilities of ongoing \nprojects, overall availability of funds and competing demands. \n \nIndian Railways(IR) constantly endeavours to improve connectivity \nbetween various parts of the country and to this end, IR introduces \nnew services, extends and increases the frequencies of existing \nservices and diverts train services, which are ongoing processes \nsubject to operational feasibility, commercial viability etc. However, \nas Railway network straddle across State boundaries , trains are \nintroduced across State boundaries as per network requirement. At \npresent, Bhagalpur is served by 62 mail/express services and 28 \npassenger services. \n \nDuring the last three years i.e. FY 2020 -21, FY 2021 -22, FY 2022 -23 \nand current FY 2023 -24, 141 Surveys (New Line, Gauge Conversion \nand Doubling) of a total length of 7217 km have been sanctioned \nand 11 Projects (New Line Gauge Conversion and Doubling) of a \ntotal  length  of  282  Kms,  costing  approx. `4164 Crore  have  been \n..P/2  \n\n-:2:- \nsanctioned which fall fully/partly in the states of West Bengal, \nJammu and Kashmir, Bihar and Madhya Pradesh. \n \nZone wise details of Railway projects, including cost, expenditure \nand outlay are made available in public domain on Indian Railways \nwebsite i.e.  www.indianrailways.gov.in> Ministry  of Railways> \nRailway Board> about Indian Railways> Railway Board \nDirectorates> Finance (Budget)> Rail Budget/Pink Book (year)> \nRailway-wise Works, Machinery and Rolling Stock Programme (RSP). \n \nWest Bengal  \nAs on 01.04.2023, 44 R ailway Infrastructure Projects (16 New line, \n04 Gauge Conversion  and 24 Doubling) of total length 4,209 km, \ncosting `50,915 crore, falling fully/partly in the state of West Bengal \nare in planning/approval/construction stage,  out  of  which, 2,033 \nkm length has been commissione d  and an expenditure of `23,298 \ncrore has been incurred upto March, 2023.  \nThese Projects falling fully/partly in the Sta te of West Bengal are \ncovered in  Eastern Railway (ER), N ortheast Frontier Railway (NFR) \nand South Eastern Railway (SER) of Indian Railways. \n \nSince 2014, there has been substantial increase in Budget \nallocation and commensurate commissioning of projects across \nIndian Railways. Average Annual Budget allocation for \nInfrastructure projects & safety works, falling fully/partly in West \nBengal is as under: \n \n..P/3 \n \n\n-:3:- \nPeriod Average Outlay \nPercentage more as \ncompared to average \nallocation during 2009-14 \n2009-14 `4380 crore/year - \n2014-23 `5621 crore/year 28% more \n2023-24 `11970 crore 173% more \n \nDuring 2014 -23, 1137 Km sections (113 km of New line, 151 Km of \nGauge Conversion and 873 km of Doubling) falling fully/partly in the \nState of West Bengal have been commissioned in the state at an \naverage rate 126.33 km per year. \n \nJAMMU & KASHMIR: \nAs on 01. 04.2023, 1 New line project covering total length of 272 \nKm, costing `37,012 crore, falling in Jammu & Kashmir has been \ntaken up out of which 161 Km length has been commissioned and \nan expenditure of `34,261 crore has been incurred upto March,2023.  \n \nRailway infrastructural Projects in the State of Jammu & Kashmir \nare covered by Northern Railway Zone of Indian Railways. \n \nAverage Annual Budget allocation for Infrastructure projects & \nsafety works, falling fully/partly in Jammu & Kashmir is as under: \nPeriod Average Outlay \nPercentage more as \ncompared to average \nallocation during 2009-14 \n2009-14 `1044 crore/year - \n2014-23 `2746 crore/year 163% more \n2023-24 `6003 crore 475% more \n  \n..P/4 \n\n-:4:- \nDuring the period 2014 -23, 39 km sections (25 km New line and 14 \nkm Doubling) falling fully/partly in Jammu & Kashmir have been \ncommissioned at an average rate 4.33 Km per year. \n \nBIHAR \nAs on 01.04.2023, 54  Railway Infrastructure Projects  (32 New \nLines, 04 Gauge Conversions and 18 Doubling), of total length of \n4,950 Km, costing `70,672 crore falling fully/partly in the State of \nBihar are in planning/approval/construction stage, out of which, \n1505 km length has been commissioned and an expe nditure of \n`25,702 crore has been incurred upto March, 2023. \n \nRailway infrastructural Projects  falling fully/partly in the State  of \nBihar are covered in  East Central Railway (ECR), Eastern Railway \n(ER), North Eastern Railway (NER), Northeast Frontier Railw ay (NFR) \nZone of Indian Railways. \n \nAverage Annual Budget allocation for Infrastructure projects & \nsafety works, falling fully/ partly in State of Bihar is as under: \nPeriod Average Outlay \nPercentage more as \ncompared to average \nallocation during \n2009-14 \n2009-14 `1132 crore/year - \n2014-23 `4006 crore/year 254% more \n2023-24 `8505 crore 651% more \n \n \n..P/5 \n  \n\n-:5:- \nDuring 2014 -23, 1308 K m sections (347 Km New Lines, 47 5 Km \nGauge Conversion and 486 Km Doubling) falling fully/partly in the \nState of Bihar has been commissioned at an average rate of 145.33 \nKm/year, which is 129% more than the commissioning during 2009 -\n14 (63.6 Km/year). \n \nMADHYA PRADESH: \nAs on 01.04.2023, 32 Railway Infrastructure Projects (08 New Lines, \n02 Gauge Conversions and 22 Doubling), of total length of 5,539 Km, \ncosting `77,797 crore falling fully/partly in Madhya Pradesh are in \nplanning/approval/construction stage, out of which 1,557 Km length \nhas been commissioned and an e xpenditure of `31,111 crore has \nbeen incurred upto March, 2023. \n \nRailway Infrastructure Projects falling fully/partly in the State of \nMadhya Pradesh are covered in  South East Central Railway, West \nCentral Railway, Central Railway, North Western Railway, Ea st \nCentral Railway, North Central Railway and Western Railway. \n  \nThe Average Annual Budget allocation for Infrastructure projects & \nsafety works, falling fully/ pa rtly in State of Madhya Pradesh is as \nunder: \nPeriod Average  \nOutlay \nPercentage more as \ncompared to average \nallocation during 2009-14 \n2009-14 `632 crore/year - \n2014-23 ` 6181 crore/year 878% more \n2023-24 `13607 crore 2053% more \n \n..P/6 \n\n-:6:- \nDuring 2014-23, 1741 km sections (293 km New line, 636 km Gauge \nconversion and 812 km Doubling), falling fully/partly in the state of \nMadhya Pradesh,  have been commissioned at an average rate of \n193.44 km per year, which is 567% more than average annual \ncommissioning achieved during 2009-14 (29 Km/Yr.). \n \n(e) and  (f) Bhind and Datia  Railway Stations are on the existing \nIndian Railways network and well connected to  both Delhi and \nBhopal. Datia and Bhind stations are also connected with each \nother via Gwalior junction.  Final Location Survey work between \nBhind-Mahoba via Orai New line has been sanctioned. Work for \nlaying 3rd line between Jhansi to Mathura via Datia (274 Km ) \ncosting `5190 crore has been taken up. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_282": "       \n \n     GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n            MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n \n    LOK SABHA \n    STARRED QUESTION NO. 282 \n     TO BE ANSWERED ON 09.08.2023 \n \n \n    INTRODUCTION OF VANDE SADHARAN TRAINS \n \n*282. SHRI HIBI EDEN: \n \n            Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the introduction of Vande Sadharan trains aligns with the \ngoal of making rail travel affordable for the common man, if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n(b) whether the upgraded second class unreserved coaches and \nsecond class 3 -tier sleeper coaches in Vande Sadharan trains will \nenhance the travel experience for passengers, if so, the details in \nthis regard; \n \n(c) whether the Government is considering any additiona l measures to \nensure timely production and deployment of Vande Sadharan trains \nacross different regions of the country, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the introduction of Vande Sadharan trains will lead to a \nsignificant reduction in ticket fares maki ng rail travel more \naffordable for the common man, if so, the details thereof and if not, \nthe reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) whether there are any specific timelines or targets set for the \ndeployment of Vande Sadharan trains in Kerala, given the region’s \nneed for budget-friendly rail travel options, if so, the details \nthereof? \n \n \n \n                                                      ANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n \n                              (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n                                            ***** \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 282 BY SHRI HIBI EDEN TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 09.08.2023 REGARDING INTRODUCTION OF VANDE \nSADHARAN TRAINS \n \n(a) to (e): Presently, Indian Railways offer among the most affordable \nservices to passengers when compared with other countries. It gave \nsubsidy of `59,837 crore on passenger tickets in 2019 -20. A variety of \ncoaches are manufactured for this purpose and new designs are \nintroduced from time to time. If Vande Sadharan refers to General \ncoaches then the position of General and Sleeper coaches is as below: \n  \nType of coaches Number of coaches \nNon - AC coaches (General & Sleeper) 41,917 \nAC coaches 20,287 \nTotal Passenger coaches 62,204 \n \n           ***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_283": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.283\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nSETTING UP OF INTEGRATED AYUSH HOSPITALS\n283. SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA:\nSHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS:\nSHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.:\nSHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI:\nSHRI VELUSAMY P.:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of integrated AYUSH hospitals/ centres set up during the last five years and the\ncurrent year along with the hospitals/centres whose construction has been completed particularly\nin Gujarat;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to set up more integrated AYUSH hospitals/centres in\neach district headquarters of the country to promote traditional system of medicines and if so, the\ndetails thereof, State/UT-wise, particularly district-wise in Tamil Nadu and Maharashtra;\n(c) the funds allocated for the said purpose during the current year, State/UT-wise;\n(d) the number of AYUSH hospitals/centres functioning in the country, State/UT-wise,\nparticularly in Odisha along with the schemes for providing AYUSH treatment facility;\n(e) the number of patients treated in the said AYUSH hospitals/centres during the last five years\nand the current year, State/UT-wise; and\n(f) the number of registered AYUSH doctors in the country and the steps taken/proposed to be\ntaken by the Government to increase such doctors?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) to (c) Public Health being a State subject, setting up of integrated Ayush hospitals/centres in\nany district of any State/UT including Gujarat, Tamil Nadu and Maharashtra, comes under the\npurview of respective State/UT Governments. However, under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of\nNational Ayush Mission (NAM), there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs for\nsetting up of 50/30/10 bedded integrated Ayush hospitals. Accordingly, State/UT Governments\nmay avail financial assistance by submitting suitable proposal through State Annual Action Plans\n(SAAPs) as per the provision of NAM guidelines. As per the proposals received from the\nState/UT Governments including Gujarat, Tamil Nadu and Maharashtra, the status of approved\n\n2\nintegrated Ayush hospitals during the last five years and the current year along with its\nconstruction status as well as approved amount are furnished as Annexure-I.\n(d) & (e) The number of Ayush hospitals/centres functioning in the country, State/UT-wise\nincluding Odisha is furnished at Annexure-II. NAM inter-alia makes provisions for the\nfollowing activities to support the State/UT Governments for providing Ayush treatment facility\nin the country including Odisha:-\n(i) Ayush Health & Wellness Centres\n(ii) Co-location of Ayush facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health\nCentres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs)\n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government Ayush Hospitals\n(iv) Upgradation of existing Government/ Panchayat/ Government aided Ayush\nDispensaries/Construction of building for existing Ayush Dispensary (Rented/dilapidated\naccommodation)/ Construction of building to establish new Ayush Dispensary\n(v) Setting up of upto 50/30/10 bedded integrated Ayush Hospitals\n(vi) Supply of essential drugs to Government Ayush Hospitals, Government Dispensaries and\nGovernment/Government aided Teaching Institutional Ayush Hospitals\n(vii) Ayush Public Health Programmes\n(viii) Infrastructural development of Ayush Under-Graduate Institutions and Ayush Post-Graduate\nInstitutions\n(ix) Establishment of new Ayush Colleges in the States where availability of Ayush teaching\ninstitutions is inadequate in the Government Sector.\nFurther, public health being State subject, treatment facility is being provided by them\nthrough existing Ayush health facilities of respective States/UTs and accordingly data of the\npatients treated are being maintained by them.\n(f) As reported by State Boards/Councils, total number of registered Ayush doctors in the\ncountry is 755780. Further, new colleges are given permission every year by NCISM (National\nCommission for Indian system of Medicine) and NCH (National Commission for Homeopathy)\nand accordingly passed out students from the colleges will get registered with concerned\nState/UT Board/Councils, so that the number of practitioners also proportionally will be\nincreased. In addition to that under NAM, there is provision of financial assistance for\nestablishment of new Ayush Colleges in the States where availability of Ayush teaching\ninstitutions is inadequate in Government Sector. The State/UT Governments may avail eligible\nfinancial assistance by projecting the same through SAAPs as per the provisions of NAM\nguidelines and it will also provide opportunities to the students for availing education of Ayush\nsystem of medicine in the States/UTs.\n\n3\nAnnexure-I\nThe status of integrated Ayush Hospitals approved and its construction status\n(Rs. in lakhs)\nS.\nNo.\nState/UT Location Amount\napproved\nduring last 5\nyears (2018-\n19 to 2022-\n23)\nAmount\napproved\n(2023-24)\nTotal\nStatus\n1 Andaman &\nNicobar\nIslands\nPort Blair 125.00 0 125.00\nUnder construction\n2 Andhra\nPradesh\nKakinada 510.00 0 510.00 Under construction\nVisakhapatnam 109.00 0 109.00 Construction not started\n3 Arunachal\nPradesh\nBhalukpong New 75.00 0 75.00 Construction not started\nLikabali New 75.00 0 75.00 Construction not started\n4 Assam Majuli District 550.00 0 550.00 Construction completed\nKokrajhar 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nBaksa 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nMorigaon 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nKaliabor 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nDiphu 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nBajali 13.00 0 13.00 Construction not started\nDudhnoi 900.00 0 900.00 Construction completed\n4 Bihar Patna 200.00 0 200.00 Under construction\n5 Chandigarh Sector -34,\nChandigarh\n289.00 0 289.00 Under construction\n6 Chhattisgarh Jangir Champa 256