    "ls|LS17_S12_U_368": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.368\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nAYUSH HOSPITALS AND PHARMACIES IN JHARKHAND\n368. SHRI JAYANTSINHA:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of AYUSH hospitals and pharmacies in Jharkhand, district-wise;\n(b) the details of the registered/licensed AYUSH practitioners in Jharkhand, district-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has any plan to enhance accessibility to AYUSH treatment facilities\nin Jharkhand, particularly in Hazaribagh and Ramgarh districts; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) The number of Ayush hospitals in Jharkhand, district-wise is furnished at Annexure-I. However, as\nreported by State Government of Jharkhand, no Government pharmacy is available in Jharkhand.\n(b) As reported by the State Government of Jharkhand, the district-wise registered/licensed Ayush\npractitioners in Jharkhand is furnished at Annexure-II.\n(c) & (d) Public Health being a State subject, enhancement of accessibility to Ayush treatment facilities\nanywhere in Jharkhand including Hazaribagh and Ramgarh districts, comes under the purview of State\nGovernment of Jharkhand. However, under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the National Ayush Mission\n(NAM), there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs including Jharkhand for promotion\nand development of Ayush system to enhance accessibility to Ayush treatment facilities. Accordingly,\nState Government may avail financial assistance by submitting suitable proposal through State Annual\nAction Plan (SAAP) as per the provision of NAM guideline. NAM inter-alia makes provisions for the\nfollowing activities:-\n(i) AyushHealth & WellnessCentres\n\n2\n(ii) Co-location of Ayush facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health Centres\n(CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs)\n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government AyushHospitals\n(iv) Upgradation of existing Government/ Panchayat/ Government aided Ayush\nDispensaries/Construction of building for existing Ayush Dispensary (Rented/dilapidated\naccommodation)/ Construction of building to establish new Ayush Dispensary\n(v) Setting up of upto 50/30/10 bedded integrated Ayush Hospitals\n(vi) Supply of essential drugs to Government Ayush Hospitals, Government Dispensaries and\nGovernment/Government aided Teaching Institutional Ayush Hospitals\n(vii) Ayush Public Health Programmes\n(viii) Infrastructural development of Ayush Under-Graduate Institutions and Ayush Post-Graduate\nInstitutions\n(ix) Establishment of new Ayush Colleges in the States where availability of Ayush teaching\ninstitutions is inadequate in the Government Sector.\n\n3\nAnnexure-I\nStatus of district-wise Ayush hospitals in Jharkhand\nS. No. Name of District Number of Ayush hospital\n1 Bokaro 1\n2 Chatra 1\n3 Deoghar 1\n4 Dhanbad 1\n5 Dumka 1\n6 Garhwa 1\n7 Giridih 1\n8 Godda 1\n9 Gumla 1\n10 Hazaribagh 1\n11 Jamtara 1\n12 Khunti 1\n13 Koderma 1\n14 Latehar 1\n15 Lohardaga 1\n16 Pakur 1\n17 Palamu 1\n18 West Singhbhum 1\n19 East Singhbhum 1\n20 Ramgarh 1\n21 Ranchi 1\n22 Sahebganj 1\n23 Saraikela 1\n24 Simdega 1\nTotal 24\n\n4\nAnnexure-II\nStatus of district-wise registered/licensed Ayush practitioners\nS. No. Name of District Homeopath\ny Ayurveda Unani Total\n1 Bokaro 46 17 2 65\n2 Chatra 15 8 0 23\n3 Deoghar 34 2 0 36\n4 Dhanbad 86 12 4 102\n5 Dumka 15 5 0 20\n6 Garhwa 81 21 6 108\n7 Giridih 41 6 0 47\n8 Godda 63 4 1 68\n9 Gumla 15 14 2 31\n10 Hazaribagh 27 15 7 49\n11 Jamtara 27 2 0 29\n12 Khunti 4 2 0 6\n13 Koderma 14 6 3 23\n14 Latehar 10 6 0 16\n15 Lohardaga 13 4 0 17\n16 Pakur 11 2 0 13\n17 Palamu 66 29 11 106\n18 East Singhbhum 173 18 9 200\n19 Ramgarh 16 14 0 30\n20 Ranchi 104 107 21 232\n21 Sahebganj 15 4 0 19\n22 Saraikela 53 10 0 63\n23 Simdega 1 2 0 3\nTotal 930 310 66 1306",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_369": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-369\nANSWERED ON- 21/07/2023\nCOMBAT ISSUE OF AIR POLLUTION\n369. DR. T. SUMATHY (A) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN\n        SHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV\nWill the Minister of  EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state :-\n(a) whether there are existing partnerships with neighboring countries to combat the issue of\nAir Pollution in South Asia, a global hotspot for the most polluted cities in the world;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, whether there are any plans to execute any such\nagreement;\n(c) whether there are any signed MoUs with neighboring countries to install air pollution\nmonitors at critical points to generate data and adopt cost-effective solutions to tackle the\nissue of air pollution based on airshed approach; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n \n(DR. RAJKUMAR RANJAN SINGH)\n(a to d) In India’s neighborhood, at the regional level, SAARC member states have signed the\n“Convention on Cooperation in Environment” in 2010. Similarly, BIMSTEC member states\nhave identified  “Environment  and  Climate  Change”  as  one  of  the  priority  areas  of\ncooperation and a draft Plan of Action for cooperation in Environment and Climate Change is\npresently under discussion among the member states. \nAt the bilateral level, India identifies cooperation activities with neighbouring countries based\non mutual consultations, mutual requirements and mutual benefits.  India has signed an\numbrella “MoU on Cooperation in the Areas of Environment” with Bhutan in June 2021 that\nenvisages long term cooperation in environmental protection and management of natural\nresources including prevention of Air Pollution. No specific cooperation agreement to combat\nair pollution have been entered into with other countries in India’s neighborhood.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_370": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.370\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nAYURVEDICHOSPITALS IN MADHYAPRADESH\n†370. SHRI GYANESHWARPATIL:\nSHRI VIVEK NARAYANSHEJWALKAR:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of Ayurvedic Hospitals/AYUSH Health Centres functioning in Madhya Pradesh,\nparticularly in Khandwa Parliamentary Constituency;\n(b) whether the Government has any special scheme for their expansion and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(c) whether the Government has any plans to establish new AYUSH hospitals/Health Centres in\nevery district of Madhya Pradesh, particularly in Khandwa Parliamentary Constituency, Gwalior\nand Shivpuri districts; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government\nin this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) The number of Ayurvedic hospitals and Ayush Health & Wellness Centres (AHWCs)\nfunctioning in Madhya Pradesh, district-wise including Khandwa district is furnished at\nAnnexure-I.\n(b) Under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the National Ayush Mission (NAM), there is provision\nof financial assistance to States/UTs including Madhya Pradesh for operationalization of AHWCs\nby upgrading Ayush dispensaries and sub health centres and setting up of upto 50/30/10 bedded\nintegrated Ayush hospitals.\n(c) & (d) Public Health being a State subject, establishment of new Ayush hospitals/Health\nCentres in any district of Madhya Pradesh, comes under the purview of State Government of\nMadhya Pradesh. However, under NAM, there is a provision of financial assistance to States/UTs\nfor setting up of 50/30/10 bedded Integrated Ayush hospitals and operationalization of AHWCs\nby upgrading Ayush dispensaries and sub health centres. Accordingly, State Government may\navail financial assistance by submitting suitable proposal through State Annual Action Plan\n(SAAP) as per the provision of NAM guidelines. Further, as per the proposals received from the\nState Government of Madhya Pradesh, the status of approved integrated Ayush Hospitals and\nAHWCs is furnished as Annexure-II.\n\n2\nAnnexure-I\nDistrict-wise status of functional Ayurvedic hospitals and Ayush Health & Wellness\nCentres (AHWCs) in Madhya Pradesh\n(i) The number of functional Ayurvedic hospitals:\nS. No. Name of the District Ayurveda\n1 Bhopal 10\n2 Sehore -\n3 Raisen -\n4 Rajgarh -\n5 Vidisha -\n6 Betul 2\n7 Hoshangabad 1\n8 Harda -\n9 Indore 3\n10 Dharwad 1\n11 Alirajpur -\n12 Jhabua 1\n13 Khargone 1\n14 Barwani -\n15 Khandwa -\n16 Burhanpur 1\n17 Jabalpur 2\n18 Katni -\n19 Chhidwara 1\n20 Seoni 1\n21 Mandla 1\n22 Balaghat 1\n23 Narsinghpur -\n24 Dindori -\n25 Gwalior 1\n26 Shivpuri 1\n27 Guna -\n28 Ashoknagar -\n29 Datia -\n30 Sheopur -\n31 Morena 1\n32 Bhind -\n33 Sagar 1\n34 Damoh 1\n35 Panna -\n36 Chhatarpur 1\n37 Tikamgarh -\n38 Ujjain 1\n39 Shajapur -\n40 Mandsaur 2\n41 Ratlam 2\n42 Dewas -\n43 Neemuch -\n\n3\n44 Agar Malwa -\n45 Rewa 1\n46 Singrauli -\n47 Sidhi 1\n48 Satna 1\n49 Umaria -\n50 Shahdol 1\n51 Anuppur -\n41\n\n4\n(ii) The number of functional Ayush Health & Wellness Centres (AHWCs):\nS. No. Name of District Functional AHWCs\n1 Agar-Malwa 3\n2 Alirajpur 5\n3 Anuppur 5\n4 Ashoknagar 8\n5 Balaghat 46\n6 Barwani 8\n7 Betul 12\n8 Bhind 16\n9 Bhopal 3\n10 Burhanpur 6\n11 Chhatarpur 18\n12 Chhindwara 12\n13 Damoh 12\n14 Datia 10\n15 Dewas 15\n16 Dhar 9\n17 Dindori 5\n18 Guna 12\n19 Gwalior 7\n20 Harda 4\n21 Hoshangabad 9\n22 Indore 9\n23 Jabalpur 13\n24 Jhabua 5\n25 Katni 15\n26 Khandwa 13\n27 Khargaon 11\n28 Mandla 8\n29 Mandsaur 11\n30 Morena 17\n31 Narsinghpur 11\n32 Neemuch 6\n33 Panna 11\n34 Raisen 7\n35 Rajgarh 9\n36 Ratlam 7\n37 Rewa 25\n38 Sagar 19\n39 Satna 22\n40 Sehore 14\n41 Seoni 11\n42 Shahdol 6\n\n5\n43 Shajapur 5\n44 Sheopur 3\n45 Shivpuri 12\n46 Sidhi 14\n47 Singrauli 7\n48 Tikamgarh 12\n49 Ujjain 16\n50 Umaria 9\n51 Vidisha 9\nTotal 562\n\n6\nAnnexure-II\n(i) The status of approved integrated Ayush hospitals\nS. No. Location of the integrated AyushHospital\n1. Bhopal\n2. Narsinghpur\n3. Indore\n4. Amarkantak\n5. Mandleshwar\n6. Balaghat\n7. Sehore\n8. Guna\n9. Panna\n10. Bhind\n(ii) The status of approved AHWCs\nSr.No. Name of District No. of AHWCs approved\n1 Ashoknagar 11\n2 Alirajpur 8\n3 Anuppur 7\n4 Agar malwa 5\n5 Balaghat 56\n6 Betul 16\n7 Bhopal 4\n8 Barwani 10\n9 Burhanpur 10\n10 Chhatarpur 21\n11 Chhindwara 18\n12 Bhind 20\n13 Datia 12\n14 Damoh 15\n15 Dhar 17\n16 Dindori 7\n17 Dewas 19\n18 Gwalior 10\n19 Guna 14\n20 Harda 6\n21 Hoshangabad 13\n22 Indore 15\n23 Jhabua 8\n24 Jabalpur 19\n25 Katni 18\n26 Khandwa 16\n27 Khargon 12\n28 Mandla 10\n29 Mandsaur 23\n30 Morena 21\n\n7\n31 Narsinghpur 15\n32 Nimuch 9\n33 Panna 15\n34 Ratlam 12\n35 Rajgarh 11\n36 Raisen 15\n37 Rewa 29\n38 Sidhi 17\n39 Sagar 28\n40 Singroli 9\n41 Satna 28\n42 Sehore 21\n43 Shajapur 6\n44 Seoni 13\n45 Sheopur 6\n46 Shivpuri 16\n47 Shahdol 9\n48 Tikamgarh 17\n49 Ujjain 22\n50 Umaria 9\n51 Vidisha 14\nTotal 762",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_371": "1\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYUSH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 371\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21/07/2023\n“SCHEME FOR CULTIV ATIONOF MEDICINAL PLANTS AND HERBS”\n371. DR. JAYANTAKUMAR ROY:\nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR:\nSMT. SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:\nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state:\na) whether the Government proposes to implement any scheme for the cultivation of natural\nherbs and medicinal plants in the country and if so, the details thereof;\nb) the details of the funds allocated for the projects under the scheme during the last three years\nand the current year, year-wise and State/UT-wise;\nc) whether the Government provides funds for development of infrastructure, research,\ndevelopment and training facilities in the AYUSH system of medicines;\nd) if so, the details thereof; and\ne) the other steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) No, Sir. However, Ministry of Ayush, Government of India had implemented Centrally\nSponsored Scheme of National Ayush Mission (NAM) to promote the cultivation of medicinal plants\nfrom the financial year 2015-16 to 2020-21. As per the scheme guidelines, the support was provided\nfor:\n(i) Cultivation of prioritized medicinal plants on farmer’s land.\n(ii) Establishment of nurseries with backward linkages for raising and supply of quality\nplanting material.\n(iii) Post-harvest management with forwarding linkages.\n(iv) Primary processing, marketing infrastructure etc.\nPresently, National Medicinal Plants Board, Ministry of Ayush, Government of India is\nimplementing Central Sector Scheme on “Conservation, Development and Sustainable Management\nof Medicinal Plants” wherein following activities are supported:\n(i) In-situ conservation / Ex-situ conservation\n\n2\n(ii) Livelihood linkages with Joint Forest Management Committees (JFMCs) / Panchayats /\nVan Panchayats / Biodiversity Management Committees (BMCs) / Self Help Groups\n(SHGs).\n(iii) IEC activities like Training / workshops / Seminars/ Conferences etc.\n(iv) Research & Development.\n(v) Promotion, marketing and trade of medicinal plants produce.\n(vi) Establishment of nurseries\n(vii) Forward and backward linkage in supply chain of medicinal plants (Integrated component)\nAdditionally, Under ICAR there is one Institute named as ICAR-Directorate of Medicinal\nand Aromatic Plant Research (ICAR-DMAPR) located at Boriavi, Anand,\nGujarat which undertakes research on basic, applied and adaptive aspect on medicinal and aromatic\nplants. It has undertaken management of Plant Genetic Resources (PGR) as a prioritized activity\nwith the effort for collection, conservation, evaluation and documentation of germplasm of\nmedicinal and aromatic plants alongwith development of Good Agricultural Practices (GAP) of\nmedicinal plants brought under cultivation and which are of high medicinal and economic value.\nFurther, DMAPR maintains several germplasm/accessions of 20 species of MAPs. Besides, large\nnumber of accessions of 54 medicinal and aromatic plants are also maintained at different ICAR-\nAICRP centres. Presently DMAPR maintains 2313 germplasm accessions of 20 species of MAPs\nand 331 accessions of Betelvine. In addition, 5060 number of accessions of 54 medicinal and\naromatic plants are maintained at 26 ICAR-All India Coordinated Research Projects (ICAR-AICRP)\ncentres at different Agro-ecological Zones of the country. Multi-location evaluation trials conducted\nunder the AICRP on Medicinal and Aromatic Plants have resulted in the identification and release of\ntwenty five new improved varieties of medicinal plants of fourteen species and seven varieties of\naromatic plants of six species.\n(b) The details of State-Wise fund allocated from the financial year 2015-16 to 2020-21 for\nmedicinal plants component under National Ayush Mission Scheme (NAM) is at Annexure-I.\n(c) to (e) Ministry of Ayush is implementing the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National\nAYUSH Mission (NAM) through State/UT Governments for the overall development and promotion\nof Ayush systems of medicine by providing financial assistance to them through different activities\nas per the provision of NAM guideline in accordance to the proposal received in their State Annual\nAction Plans (SAAPs). Under NAM, there are provisions for financial assistance for infrastructure\nstrengthening under following activities : -\n(i) Ayush Health & Wellness Centres.\n(ii) Co-location of Ayush facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs),\nCommunity Health Centres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs).\n(iii) Upgradation of existing standalone Government Ayush Hospitals.\n(iv) Upgradation of existing Government/Panchayat/Government aided Ayush\nDispensaries/ Construction of building for existing Ayush Dispensary (Rented/\n\n3\ndilapidated accommodation)/ Construction of building to establish new Ayush\nDispensary in the area where there are no Ayush facilities available.\n(v) Setting up of upto 10/30/50 bedded integrated Ayush Hospitals.\n(vi) Establishment of new Ayush colleges in the States where availability of\nAYUSH teaching institutions is inadequate in Government Sector.\n(vii) Infrastructural development of Ayush Under-Graduate Institutions and\nPost-Graduate Institutions/ add on PG/ Pharmacy /Para-Medical Courses.\nGovernment of India has setup Central Council for Research in Ayurvedic Sciences (CCRAS)\nan autonomous organization for undertaking, coordinating, formulating, developing and promoting\nresearch on scientific lines in Ayurveda. The research activities are carried out through 30\ninstitutes/Centres/Units located all over India and through collaborative studies with various\nuniversities, hospitals and institutes. Budget allocated is as under:\nYear 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23\nBudget (Rs. in Crore) 272.62 316.97 360.50\nMinistry of Ayush provides funds for development of infrastructure, research, development\nand training facilities in the Siddha system of medicines viz intramural research projects (IMR),\nprovision of state of art infrastructure with sophisticated instruments, unit for performing optimal\nresearch and development of Siddha system of medicine.\nDetails of fund utilised for development of infrastructure, research, development for the past\nthree years (in crores):\nYear 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23\nBudget (Rs. in\nCrore)\n35.30 39.06 46.88\nSteps taken by CCRS to promote Siddha medicinal research and related activities:\n Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS) is the apex body of research in Siddha\nsystem of Medicine. CCRS undertakes the scientific validation of the Siddha System of\nMedicine through clinical research, drug research, medicinal plants research, fundamental\nresearch, literary research and documentation.\n Central Council for Research in Siddha has granted 75 IMR projects and out of this 8 IMR\nprojects have been completed.\n 54 books, 10 Patents and 650 scientific research articles have been published by CCRS.\n CCRS is equipped with state of the art infrastructure with sophisticated instruments and\nfacilities in the following Departments: Department of Clinical Research, Department of Bio-\nchemistry, Department of Pathology, Radiology, Department of Chemistry, Department of\nPharmacognosy etc., to carry out IMR projects and other associated research work.\n\n4\n The Council is engaged in multifaceted research activities. In the area of clinical research,\npre-clinical Pharmacological / Toxicological studies, open label trials, multi-centric\nRandomized Controlled Trials (RCTs) are being conducted for acute and chronic diseases.\nThe main emphasis is on diseases of national health priority with a view to develop safe and\neffective Siddha treatments.\n In addition, an MoU with Accelerating Growth of New India Innovation (AGNIi) for the\ncommercialization of R&D technologies and products developed by CCRS.\n Council is also engaged in national collaboration with reputed scientific organization and\nmedical institutions / Universities on diseases of national priorities.\nAdditionally, National Institute of Siddha is an autonomous body under the Ministry of\nAyush that conducts UG degree, PG degree and Ph.D., in Siddha. Ministry of Ayush has sanctioned\nRs. 37 crore under capital head for infrastructure development and Rs.38.29 crore under general\nhead for Institute’s activities including research, development and training facilities under Siddha\nsystem of Medicine for the financial year 2023-2024.\nGovernment of India has setup Central Council for Research in Unani Medicine (CCRUM)\nand allocated Rs. 175.05 Crore for development of infrastructure and research in the Unani system\nof medicines during the financial year 2022-23.\nCentral Council for Research in Homoeopathy is an apex body for scientific research in\nhomoeopathy under the Ministry of AYUSH undertakes research activities such as clinical research,\ndrug proving, drug verification, drug standardization, fundamental research and epidemic research\neither in intramural mode or in collaboration with different institutes of repute. CCRH has been\nfunded centrally by the Ministry of Ayush and the expenditure is centrally managed. The funds\nallocated during the last three years and the current year is given below:\nYear 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23\nAllocation 130.50 Cr. 143.58 Cr. 143.70 Cr.\nCCRH has strengthened its infrastructure in terms of having own buildings, developing new\nlaboratories, upgrading of existing laboratories in terms of equipment for undertaking high end\nresearch studies in the area of clinical, pre-clinical/ fundamental/ basic research.\n*******\n\n5\nAnnexure-I\nThe details of funds allocated to the states under Medicinal Plants Component of “National\nAyush Mission” (NAM) Scheme from the Financial Year2015-16 to 2020-21\n(Rs. in lakh)\nS.\nNo\nName of State 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 Total\n1 Andhra Pradesh 133.924 193.165 213.158 128.600 317.00 305.50 1291.35\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 25.032 45.031 61.111 36.900 48.70 59.90 276.67\n3 Assam 158.971 175.370 392.284 236.700 313.90 453.90 1731.13\n4 Andaman & Nicobar 19.489 40.802 68.922 41.600 43.40 50.30 264.51\n5 Bihar 396.061 691.887 659.830 398.200 857.30 1258.60 4261.88\n6 Chandigarh 92.554 36.445 88.397 53.300 36.70 45.40 352.80\n7 Chhattisgarh 152.705 217.942 178.646 107.800 343.70 467.80 1468.59\n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 38.000 58.619 43.581 26.300 22.90 33.20 222.60\n9 Daman & Diu 48.819 69.048 38.721 23.400 22.90 202.89\n10 Delhi 52.860 45.422 152.470 55.200 68.60 51.10 425.65\n11 Gujarat 168.141 219.886 185.050 111.700 380.40 547.50 1612.68\n12 Goa 69.862 41.606 38.115 23.000 38.10 46.40 257.08\n13 Haryana 106.131 122.627 166.010 100.200 342.90 306.00 1143.87\n14 Himachal Pradesh 62.115 60.036 75.783 45.700 128.90 142.70 515.23\n15 Jammu & Kashmir 59.336 93.276 80.392 48.500 106.50 66.60 454.60\n16 Jharkhand 144.140 262.674 235.537 142.100 441.10 454.60 1680.15\n17 Karnataka 137.270 233.208 247.465 149.300 243.40 408.80 1419.44\n18 Kerala 126.562 169.608 269.787 162.800 237.00 405.30 1371.06\n19 Ladakh 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.00 28.50 28.50\n20 Lakshadweep 37.478 58.104 27.971 16.910 22.90 25.90 189.26\n21 Madhya Pradesh 306.973 499.847 450.651 271.900 564.90 970.90 3065.17\n22 Maharashtra 205.972 373.846 355.725 214.700 413.90 599.00 2163.14\n23 Manipur 60.896 73.380 90.341 54.500 46.40 84.60 410.12\n24 Meghalaya 38.757 55.348 91.576 55.300 46.10 65.20 352.28\n25 Mizoram 43.806 44.345 97.568 58.900 25.40 43.30 313.32\n26 Nagaland 60.502 53.493 104.669 63.200 82.80 75.50 440.16\n27 Orissa 187.726 308.835 297.518 179.500 356.70 521.80 1852.08\n28 Punjab 89.625 150.301 104.201 62.900 148.40 219.60 775.03\n29 Puducherry 20.075 23.191 38.115 13.800 22.90 20.70 138.78\n30 Rajasthan 264.413 474.860 503.220 303.700 533.40 832.80 2912.39\n31 Sikkim 30.789 32.172 25.410 15.300 25.40 42.20 171.27\n32 Tamil Nadu 144.452 252.725 286.666 173.000 306.00 470.20 1633.04\n33 Telangana 111.350 150.835 119.135 71.900 177.90 323.10 954.22\n34 Tripura 36.864 54.522 77.281 46.600 59.30 77.80 352.37\n\n6\n35 Uttar Pradesh 720.119 1241.326 1238.718 753.300 1526.00 2278.20 7757.66\n36 Uttarakhand 60.989 70.024 130.148 78.500 182.20 212.60 734.46\n37 West Bengal 180.811 318.860 348.760 210.500 397.90 638.30 2095.13\nTotal 4593.57 7012.666 7582.932 4535.710 8931.900 12633.80 45290.58",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_372": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \n \n \nLOK     S A B H A \n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 372 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 21st JULY, 2023. \n \nEffect of UCC on other Laws \n \n \n372. SHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \n PROF.  SOUGATA RAY: \n SHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \n SHRI RAKESH SINGH. \n  \n  \n Will the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether Uniform Civil Code (UCC) is being drafted to be implemented in the \ncountry and if so, the details and the time -frame thereof along with the stand of the \nGovernment thereon;  \n(b)  whether various religious groups have been  taken into confidence regardin g its \nimplementation, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether adoption of cultures, beliefs and  traditions of tribal community is a \nchallenging factor for UCC and may cause difficulties in its implementation; \n(d)   if so, the details thereof along with the measures proposed to keep them out of the \nambit of UCC; \n(e)  whether the Government has analysed the  likely effect of implementation of UCC \non other laws and if so, the details thereof and the steps proposed  to be taken by the \nGovernment to address such situations; and \n(f) whether  the UCC is likely to be a single code  for everyone like Cr.PC and IPC and \nif so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE; \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS; AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CULTURE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n(a) to (f) : The Government had  requested the 21 st  Law Commission of India to \nundertake examination of various issues relating to uniform civil code and to \nmake recommendations thereon. The term of the 21 st Law Commission ended on \n31.8.2018 and the 22nd Law Commission took up the reference on uniform civil \ncode for consideration and solicited views and ideas of the public at large and \nrecognized organizations through a public notice dated 14.6.2023.   Stake holders \nare at liberty to submit their comments to Law Commission till the 28 th of  July, \n2023. \n                                                          **** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_373": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.373\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY,2023\nRADICAL CHANGES IN HEALTHCARE SYSTEM\n373: SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAYBHATT:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is considering to bring radical changes in the healthcare\nsystem and if so, the details thereof; and\n(b) whether the Government has taken/proposes to take any steps in this regard, if so, the\ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (b) : The National Health Policy 2017 envisages to provide universal access to good\nquality health care services through increasing access, increasing affordability by lowering the\ncost of healthcare delivery and equity. The goal of the policy is to attain the highest possible\nlevel of health and wellbeing for all at all ages through a preventive and promotive health care\norientation in all developmental policies and universal access to good quality health care services\nwithout anyone having to face financial hardship as a consequence.\nThe Government has launched four mission mode projects, namely PM-Ayushman Bharat Health\nInfrastructure Mission (PM-ABHIM), Ayushman Bharat Health & Wellness Centres (ABHWCs),\nPradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PMJAY) and Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM).\nOther notable initiatives are National Health Mission (NHM), setting up of new AIIMS and\nupgrading of Government medical colleges under the Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojna\n(PMSSY), support to State/UTs for setting up of new medical colleges as well as for increasing\nUG and PG medical seats , provision of free drugs and free diagnostic facilities at Primary\nHealth Centres (PHC)/ Community Health Centres (CHC) and district hospital level, substantial\nincrease in availability of government ambulances etc. The Fifteenth Finance Commission has\nalso recommended health grants aggregating to Rs. 70,051 crore to local government for\naddressing the gaps in primary health infrastructure in rural and urban areas.\nBesides, quality generic medicines are made available at affordable prices to all under\nPradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) in collaboration with the State\nGovernments. Affordable Medicines and Reliable Implants for Treatment (AMRIT) Pharmacy\nstores have been set up in some hospitals/institutions.\n***",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_374": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 374\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nCASES OF HEAT WA VEDEATHS\n374. SHRI RAMALINGAM S.:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps or remedial measures to prevent heat wave\ndeaths in the country, particularly in Tamil Nadu;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has any clear data on the deaths reported due to heat wave across\nthe country during the last 10 years, particularly in the State of Tamil Nadu and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(d) whether the Government has any policy/data/action plan for reducing the heat wave deaths in\nthe country; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e):\nUnion Health Ministry has launched National Programme on Climate Change & Human Health\n(NPCCHH) in 2019 with an objective to create awareness, strengthen capacity of healthcare system\nand health preparedness & response, and synergy/partnership with other missions to mitigate the\nimpact of climate change on human health.\nUnder National Programme on Climate Change and Human Health (NPCCHH), Union Health\nMinistry has taken several initiatives focused on providing technical support to states to address\nheat wave related issues. These include:\n\n1. The State officials has been advised to disseminate messages to increase general\nawareness through various IEC activities and conduct sensitization workshops on heat’s\nhealth impact and its prevention.\n2. To strengthen the public healthcare system, NPCCHH conducts capacity building\ntrainings/workshops of State/District level programme officers, health care professionals\nand health workers on tracking and managing of heat-related health issues.\n3. Multiple advisories on health sector preparedness, including do’s and don’ts are also\nshared with states.\n4. \"National Action Plan on Heat Related Illnesses\" has been released in July, 2021 and also\navailable on the website of Union Ministry of Health & Family Welfare.\n5. States are further advised to prepare their state-level heat health sector plan with focus on\nawareness building, health system capacity upgradation and strengthening of health\ninfrastructure to treat heat related illness and reduce related deaths. Till date, 28 States\nhave completed their heat-health action plans including State Government of Tamil Nadu.\n6. India Meteorological Department (IMD) issues heatwave alerts (early warning) and these\nare further shared with State Nodal Officers-Climate Change to ensure health facility\npreparedness and follow up.\n7. To ensure adequate preparedness at State/UT level ,Union Health Ministry has released\nGuidelines on Strengthening Health System Preparedness for Heat-Related Illnesses (HRI)\nincluding preparedness, control and management in April, 2023.\nDeaths as reported by States/UTs due to heat wave is at Annexure.\n******\n\nAnnexure\nState/UTs-wise list of deaths due to heat wave\nStates/UTs 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022\n2023\n(Till\n30.06.2023)\nAndhra Pradesh 1422 723 236 8 29 0 0 1 4\nArunachal Pradesh -- -- -- -- NR 1 NR 0 0\nBihar -- 0 0 2 121 NR NR 0 8\nChhattisgarh -- 1 0 0 0 0 NR NR 0\nDelhi -- -- 0 0 0 NR 0 0 1\nGoa -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR 0 0\nGujarat 17 7 0 3 8 NR NR 0 35\nHaryana 0 0 0 0 0 NR NR NR 0\nHimachal Pradesh -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR NR 9\nJammu & Kashmir\n(Jammu Division) -- -- -- --\n0 1 NR\n0 0\nJammu & Kashmir\n(KashmirDivision)\n0 2 NR\nJharkhand -- 4 0 0 0 NR NR NR 0\nKarnataka -- 0 2 0 0 NR 0 NR 1\nKerala -- -- -- -- 1 0 0 0 120\nMadhya Pradesh -- 0 3 1 2 NR NR NR 10\nMaharashtra -- 7 9 2 6 0 NR 27 14\nOdisha 60 36 17 7 0 NR 0 3 7\nPunjab 0 0 0 0 0 NR NR 0 0\nRajasthan -- 9 9 1 3 0 NR NR 0\nTamil Nadu 0 0 0 0 1 NR 0 0 12\nTelangana 541 324 108 0 42 NR NR 2 20\nUttar Pradesh -- 0 0 0 2 NR 0 NR 12\nUttarakhand -- -- -- -- 0 NR NR 0 0\nWest Bengal -- 0 0 1 NR NR NR NR 11",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_375": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 375\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023\nMORTALITYDUE TO RABIES\n375. ADV .DEAN KURIAKOSE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTHAND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the fatalities due to Rabies infection caused by dog bite and if so, the details\nthereof, State/UT-wise;\n(b) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to improve the availability of anti-rabies\nvaccines/medicines in the Government hospitals in Kerala;\n(c) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to control/curb stray dogs in Kerala;\n(d) whether the Government is likely to empower the local self-government to cull stray dogs\nas a means to control attack by them; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILYWELFARE\n(PROF. SATYAPAL SINGH BAGHEL)\n(a) to (e):\nUnion Health Ministry has launched the National Rabies Control Programme (NRCP) for prevention and\ncontrol of Rabies. To effectively control rabies, ‘National Action Plan For Dog Mediated Rabies\nElimination (NAPRE) from India by 2030’ has been launched by Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\nalongwith Ministry of Fisheries Animal Husbandry & Dairying, Government of India. As reported by\nStates/UTs in IDSP , State/UT-wise details of the fatalities due to rabies infection is at Annexure I.\nAs per the information provided by the State Government of Kerala, Vaccines are procured through Kerala\nMedical Services Corporation (KMSCL) through open tender process . The stock position of vaccines and\nimmunoglobulins are being monitored on a daily basis at district and state levels so that available vaccines\nare distributed judiciously. If necessary, rabies biologicals are also mobilised from neighbouring states\n\nsuch as Tamilnadu. In cases of emergency, institutions can also purchase vaccines/immunoglobulins\ndirectly from the open market using local purchase mechanisms.\nAs informed by Govt of Kerala several initiatives have been taken to control stray dogs in Kerala\nincluding :\ni. Government has initiated concerted efforts involving all stakeholders including the key\ndepartments of Local Self Governance Institute(LSGI) , Animal Husbandry and Health.\nii. Intersectoral meetings have been convened by the respective Ministers with decisions to undertake\nanimal birth control activities, mass dog vaccination, ensuring infrastructure & anti- rabies\nimmunologicals in hospitals and intense IEC activities.\niii. IEC activities have been intensified in print, audio-visual and social media regarding need for\nwound washing & post-exposure prophylaxis after animal bites, responsible pet ownership, pet\nvaccination and adequate waste management.\niv. Animal Birth Control (ABC) activities have been intensified by the LSGI department with the help\nof Animal Husbandry department. Guidelines have been issued for undertaking the ABC activities\nwith LSGI project funds and includes training of dog-catchers and setting up of infrastructure.\nv. Mass dog vaccination (pet dogs & stray dogs) have been ongoing during the months of September,\nOctober and November 2022 and mass dog vaccination drive are conducted every year during the\nmonth of September.\nvi. Capacity buildings of health-care workers are being conducted on an ongoing basis in all districts\nregarding first aid, wound care, categorization of wounds, post exposure prophylaxis and\nadministration of intradermal vaccine & immunoglobulins.\nvii. Rabies has been included as a Notifiable Disease under the provisions of the Kerala Public Health\nOrdinance 2022.\nviii.Joint Steering Committee for preparation of State Action Plan for Elimination of Dog Mediated\nRabies (SAPRE) has been constituted under the Chairmanship of Chief Secretary and the first\nmeeting was held on 16th November, 2022.\nAccording to State Government of Kerala, culling of stray dogs as a means to control dog bites are not\nallowed as per existing rules\n*****\n\nAnnexure I\nState/UT-wise details of the fatalities due to Rabies infection\nState/UTs 2022\nAndhra Pradesh 29\nArunachal Pradesh 1\nAssam 12\nBihar 21\nChandigarh 0\nChhattisgarh 2\nDadar Nagar Haveli & Daman Diu 0\nDelhi 48\nGoa 1\nGujarat 6\nHaryana 0\nHimachal Pradesh 1\nJammu 4\nJharkhand 4\nKarnataka 29\nKashmir 1\nKerala 27\nMadhya Pradesh 4\nMaharashtra 29\nManipur 2\nMeghalaya 0\nMizoram 0\nNagaland 0\nPuducherry 0\nOdisha NA\nPunjab 2\nRajasthan 0\nSikkim 0\nTamilnadu 20\nTelangana 21\nTripura 0\nUT Ladakh 0\nUttar Pradesh 5\nUttarakhand 0\nWest Bengal 38",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_376": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 376 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2023 \n  \nProduction of Generic Drugs  \n \n†376. SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the share of India in global trade of generic drugs;  \n(b) the quantum and value of generic drugs produced during the last three years and the current \nyear along with the value and quantity of such p roducts which have been exported and \nimported, category-wise and year-wise;  \n(c) the respective share of generic and nongeneric drugs in total sale of drugs in India including \nthose sold in Government hospitals; and \n(d) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to promote the use of generic drugs \nincluding the mandatory provision for physicians to provide prescription of generic drugs and \nthe outcome of these steps?  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n  \n(a) to (c):  Indian Pharmaceuticals Industry is 3rd largest by volume and 13th largest by value in \nthe world. It is one of the largest supplier of generic medicines with  about 20% share in the \nglobal supply and produces more tha n 60,000 generic drugs across 60 therapeutic categories. \nThe data pertaining to turnover, export and import of the Pharmaceutical sector during the last \nthree years is as under:  \nRs. in crore \nFinancial Year Turnover Export Imports \n2019-2020 2,89,998 1,40,537 40,139 \n2020-2021 3,28,054 1,74,064 46,808 \n2021-2022 3,44,125 1,74,955 60,060 \n \nData pertaining specifically to generic drugs is, however, not maintained.   \n \n(d): In order to provide quality generic medicines at affordable prices to all citizens, especially \nthe poor and the deprived ones, Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana  (PMBJP) \nhas been launched by the Department of Pharmaceuticals wherein about 9,512 dedicated outlets \nknown as Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras  (PMBJKs) have been opened till \n30.06.2023. \n \nFurther, Clause 1.5 of Indian Medical Council (Professional Conduct, Etiquette and \nEthics) Regulations, 2002 prescribes that every physician should prescribe drugs with generic \n\nnames legibly and preferably in capital letters and shall ensure that there is a rational \nprescription and use of drug. Registered Medical Practitioners have been directed by the \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare from time to time to comply with the aforesaid \nprovisions. States have also been advised by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare to ensure \nprescription of generic drugs and conduct regular prescription audits in public health facilities. \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_377": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nDEPARTMENTOF HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †377\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY,2023\nMEDICAL COLLEGES RECOGNIZED BY MEDICAL COUNCIL OF INDIA\n†377. SHRI RAMCHARAN BOHRA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILYWELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number and name of medical colleges recognized by the Medical Council of India (MCI)\nin the country during the last three years and the current year;\n(b) the funds allocated by the Government to such recognized medical colleges by MCI for\nproviding facilities, college-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has made any assessment regarding the reforms and the progress\nmade in the construction of buildings, purchase of medical equipments, teaching facilities\netc.; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATEIN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY\nWELFARE\n(DR. BHARATI PRA VINPAWAR)\n(a) to (d): The Government has increased number of medical colleges and subsequently\nincreased MBBS seats. There is an increase of 82% in Medical Colleges from 387 before 2014 to\n704 as of now. Further, there is an increase of 110% in MBBS seats from 51,348 before 2014 to\n107,948 as of now, there is also an increase of 117% in PG seats from 31,185 before 2014 to\n67,802 as of now.\nAs informed by National Medical Commission (NMC) (erstwhile Medical Council of\nIndia (MCI)), a total of 183 Medical College have been recognised by the MCI/ NMC under the\nIndian Medical Council Act, 1956/ National Medical Commission Act, 2019 during the last three\nyears and current year. States/ UT-wise details are at Annexure.\n\nUnder CSS for strengthening/ upgradation of existing State Government/ Central\nGovernment Medical Colleges to increase the number of MBBS (UG) and PG seats, under which\nsupport has been provided for increase of 4677 MBBS seats in 77 colleges with an approved cost\nof Rs. 5612.25 Cr, 4058 PG seats in phase-I in 72 colleges with an approved cost of 1498.43 Cr\nand 3957 PG seats in phase -II in 62 colleges with an approved cost of Rs. 4461.44 Cr.\nUnder Upgradation of Government Medical Colleges by construction of Super-Specialty\nBlocks of Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) Scheme a total of 75 projects\nhave been approved, of which 62 projects are complete. This Ministry monitors the progress of\nthe projects through various review meetings held with the States/ UTs.\n\nAnnexure\nDetails of Medical Colleges recognized during the last three years\nS\nNo.\nState/UT\nMedical Colleges recognized\n2020 2021 2022 2023\n1 Andhra Pradesh 2 5 0 4\n2 Assam 0 0 0 0\n3 Andaman & Nicobar 1 0 0 1\n4 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 1\n5 Bihar 2 0 1 0\n6 Chhattisgarh 2 4 0 3\n7 Delhi 0 1 1 0\n8 Gujarat 2 4 0 5\n9 Haryana 2 3 1 4\n10 Himachal Pradesh 0 1 0 4\n11 Karnataka 3 6 3 6\n12 Kerala 0 3 2 2\n13 Madhya Pradesh 1 2 0 5\n14 Maharashtra 3 5 6 2\n15 Manipur 0 0 1 0\n16 Mizoram 0 0 0 1\n17 Odisha 0 1 0 3\n18 Punjab 0 1 0 0\n19 Rajasthan 1 4 0 7\n20 Tamil Nadu 1 4 4 2\n21 Telangana 0 6 0 6\n22 Tripura 0 0 1 0\n23 Uttar Pradesh 6 11 2 10\n24 Uttarakhand 0 1 0 1\n25 West Bengal 0 4 1 1",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_378": "Government of India \nMinistry of Chemicals & Fertilizers \nDepartment of Chemicals & Petrochemicals \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTON NO- 378 \nANSWERED ON -21/07/2023 \n  \nCONSUMPTION OF AUS-40 and AUS-32 \n  \n378. SHRI KESINENI SRINIVAS \n  \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state :-  \n \n(a) the details of the quantity of annual consumption of AUS-40 and AUS-32; \n(b) whether the Government maintains any list of the procurement source of AUS-40 and AUS-\n32; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government maintains any list of the procurement source of imported AUS-40 \nand AUS-32; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof?         \n \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n  \n(a-e) The Chemicals and Petrochemicals is a de-licensed and deregulated sector in \ngeneral. Department of Chemicals and Petrochemicals monitors the production and \nconsumption of only the limited number of chemicals and petrochemicals; as such the  data \nabout the consumption and procurement sources of domestic and imported AUS32 and AUS40 \nare not maintained. \n  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_379": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.379 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2023 \n \nCENTRALLY SPONSORED SCHEMES \n \n379.  DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of Centrally Sponsored Schemes and Central Sector Schemes being \nimplemented by the Ministry in the State of West Bengal during the last four years \nand the current year, year-wise, scheme-wise and district-wise including North and \nSouth Dinajpur districts;  \n(b) the details of funds allocated, sanctioned, released and utilized for each of the \nabove schemes during the last four years and the current year, year-wise, scheme-\nwise and district-wise;  \n(c) the details of physical targets set and achiev ed while implementing the above \nschemes during the last five years and the current year, year -wise, scheme-wise \nand district-wise;  \n(d) whether the Ministry has found any shortcomings while implementing the above \nschemes and if so, the details thereof including the steps taken by the Government \nto check those shortcomings;  \n(e) whether there is time and cost overrun in any of the above schemes; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) For better implementation and efficient monitoring, all Centrally Sponsored \nSchemes of the Ministry being implemented in the country, including West Bengal, have \nbeen clubbed into three verticals, viz. (1) Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0 \nfor improving nutrition indicators in the country (2) Mission Shakti, for safety, protection \nand empowerment of women; and (3) Mission Vatsalya, for protection and welfare of \nchildren. The details of these schemes are as under: \n  \n(i) Saksham Anganwadi & Poshan 2.0:  This programme has been reorganized into 3 \nprimary verticals: (i) Nutrition Support for POSHAN and for Adolescent Girls (ii) Early \nChildhood Care and Education [3 -6 years] and (iii) Anganwadi Infrastructure including \nmodern, upgraded Saksham Anganwadis.  \n  \n(ii) Mission Shakti: Mission Shakti comprises of two sub -schemes ‘Sambal’ and \n‘Samarthya’ for safety and security of women and empowerment of women respectively. \nThe existing schemes of One Stop Centres (OSC), Women Helplines (181-WHL) and  \n\n \n \n \n \nBeti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) have been made part of Sambal sub - scheme; while \nthe existing schemes of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY), Ujjwala, \nSwadhar Greh and Working Women Hostel and National Creche Scheme have been \nsubsumed in ‘Samarthya’. \n  \n(iii) Mission Vatsalya: Under Mission Vatsalya, the Child Protection Scheme(CPS) has \nbeen strengthened for better outreach and protection for children in need and care in a \nmission mode with the objective to: (i)Support and sustain Children in difficult \ncircumstances. (ii) Develop context -based solutions for holistic development of children \nfrom varied backgrounds. (iii) Provide scope for green field projects for encouraging \ninnovative solutions. (iv) Cement convergent action by gap funding, if required.  \n  \nOnly under 3 schemes of Mission Shakti, funds were being directly released to districts \nof all the States/UTs viz. One Stop Centre (OSC), Universalization of Women Helplines \n(WHL) and Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) till FY 2022-23. However, BBBP & WHL are \nnot being implemented by State Government of West Bengal. District -wise details of \nfunds allocated/ released and utilized under the OSC scheme during the last four years \nis annexed at Annexure-I. In the rest of the schemes, the funds are being released to \nStates/UTs. Details of funds allocated/ released and utilized under the schemes during \nthe last four years and the current year is annexed at Annexure-II. \n  \n(c)  The physical targets and achievements in West Bengal under schemes of the \nMinistry are annexed at Annexure-III.  \n  \n(d), (e) & (f) PMMVY is being implemented in the State of West Bengal since 2017 - 18. \nHowever, it had come to the notice that the Scheme was being implemented in the State \nwith a different nomenclature i.e. 'Bangla Matri Prakalpa'. The State government later on \nagreed and ensured that in future the scheme would be implemented strictly as per the \nguidelines of PMMVY. On receipt of the written assurance from the State, the release of \nCentral share of funds to the State Government of West Bengal has been resumed from \nMarch, 2022. \n  \nUnder all the schemes of the Ministry, Utilisation Certificate/Statement of Expenditure are \nnot being received from the State. Regular follow up meetings have been conducted with \nthe State at all levels to report the utilisation of funds. \n \nAll the schemes of the Ministry were evaluated by NITI Aayog and their suggestions were \nincorporated while reorganising the schemes of the Ministry into 3 Missions. \n  \n*****  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n  \nDistrict-wise details of funds allocated/ released and utilized in West Bengal \nunder schemes of the Ministry during the last four years \n  \nI. One Stop Centre -  \n(Rs. in Lakhs) \nS. \nNo. \nDistrict 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nReleased Utilised Released Utilised Released Utilised Released Utilised \n1 Alipurduar 0 0 0 0 0 0 28.41 0 \n2 Bankura 0 0 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 \n3 Birbhum 15.91 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n4 \nCooch Behar \n0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0 0 \n5 Dakshin Dinajpur(S\nouth_ \n0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 \nDarjelling \n15.91 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 Hooghly 15.91 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Howrah 15.91 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Jalpaiguri 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Jhargam 0 0 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 \n11 Kalimpong 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0 0 \n12 Kolkata 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n13 Malda 0 0 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 \n14 Murshidabad 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0 0 \n15 Nadia 0 0 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 \n16 North 24 Parghana\ns \n0 0 0 0 28.41 0 0 0 \n17 Paschim Bardhama\nn(West) \n0 0 28.41 0 0 0 0